<h2>The ingredients, precisely</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A parent unable to maintain themselves.</strong> Inability is relative, not destitution: the question is whether the parent can sustain a reasonable living from their own income and assets. A small pension does not by itself defeat the claim if it cannot cover real needs, including medical care.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A child with sufficient means.</strong> Means includes earning capacity. The obligation falls on sons and daughters alike; adoptive parents are covered, and the mother&rsquo;s claim does not depend on the father&rsquo;s.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Neglect or refusal.</strong> Demonstrated by demand and non-response, abandonment, or exclusion from the home. A formal written demand before filing, though not mandatory, makes the neglect easy to prove.</div>
</div>

<h2>Procedure before the Magistrate</h2>
<p>The application lies before a Judicial Magistrate of the first class in a district where the applicant or respondent resides. The proceeding is summary: affidavits of means, disclosures, focused evidence. Interim maintenance can be sought during pendency, and under the BNSS the endeavour is to dispose of interim applications within sixty days of notice. Orders operate from the date of order or, where the court so directs, from the date of application &mdash; a point worth pressing where the matter has dragged.</p>

<h2>Section 144 BNSS vs the Senior Citizens Act, 2007</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Section 144 BNSS</th><th>Senior Citizens Act, 2007</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Forum</td><td>Judicial Magistrate</td><td>Maintenance Tribunal (executive)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Who can be ordered to pay</td><td>Children with means</td><td>Children; for childless senior citizens, relatives who possess or would inherit their property</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monthly ceiling</td><td>None — fixed on needs and means</td><td>Capped as notified under the Act</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Property remedies</td><td>None directly</td><td>Transfer made on condition of maintenance can be declared void at the transferor&rsquo;s option when the condition fails</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Fine-recovery machinery and up to a month&rsquo;s imprisonment per unpaid month</td><td>Tribunal recovery; district machinery</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Three drafting notes for practitioners</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Plead the medical budget.</strong> For elderly applicants, recurring medical expenditure is usually the largest head and the most document-rich; itemise it rather than folding it into a lump sum.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Name all earning children.</strong> Apportionment arguments collapse when only one child is sued; impleading all with means forestalls the &ldquo;why only me&rdquo; defence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Watch the one-year arrears window.</strong> Enforcement applications for unpaid instalments must be made within a year of each amount falling due — diarise it, because sympathy does not extend limitation.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory scheme in general terms for information. It is not legal advice; the right forum and strategy depend on each family&rsquo;s circumstances.</div>
